WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.370
- [Narrator] This half gallon
of whole milk from Aldi

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.890
costs $2.18 cents,

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.570
eggs 1.87, bread 1.29.
(gentle music)

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.490
Aldi is the fastest-growing grocer

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.140
by store count in the country,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.520
and its no-frills approach

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.718
has also made it one of the cheapest.

00:00:16.718 --> 00:00:18.210
(trolley thudding)
For the past decade,

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.390
it's been growing at a rate
of about 100 stores per year.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.600
And when an economic
downturn hits, it thrives.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.060
- It's about simplicity,
and it's about efficiency,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.730
and it's about a consistent
experience all the time.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.037
- [Narrator] This is
"The Economics of Aldi."

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.020
So how does the company
keep its prices so low?

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.820
Take its store set up.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:39.720
- You walk in,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.610
and the moment you step foot in our store,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.545
you can see all four walls.

00:00:43.545 --> 00:00:46.410
- [Narrator] Aldi's are typically
around 12,000 square feet,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.870
about the same as a Trader Joe's,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.690
and much smaller than
the average supermarket.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.190
Operating a smaller space

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.200
saves money when buying the property

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.300
on the lease and on utilities.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.980
- The layout at Aldi is as much designed

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.110
for our operational
efficiency as anything.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.800
And so being very specific
about the amount of footsteps

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.110
it takes to service our store

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.480
and a very limited store size

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.810
allows us to operate a
very efficient operation,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.910
and that creates a lot of value.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.350
- [Narrator] To further its efficiency,

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.240
Aldi doesn't have extra services

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.590
like a customer help desk,

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.480
a fresh bakery, or butcher,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.600
and its stocks significantly
fewer products.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.380
Supermarkets typically carry
around 31,000 products.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.750
Trader Joe's has about 4,000,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.320
but Aldi stocks about 1,600.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.390
Often, there are just
three to five employees

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:37.620
on the sales floor,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.940
so having fewer items

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.490
and leaving them in the
boxes they were delivered in

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.300
means employees spend less
time stocking shelves.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.040
These cost-cutting strategies

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.530
are critical to Aldi's profit margin,

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:52.110
and they're why the company
is able to offer items

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.690
that are cheaper than
the national average,

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.390
but they also serve another purpose.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.350
They help maintain Aldi's
image as a discount store.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.840
Customers make compensatory inferences.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.707
Basically, marketing experts say

00:02:05.707 --> 00:02:07.830
"When people see an item's price,

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.740
they quickly make assumptions
about the product.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.100
If it's cheap, they may
assume it's lower quality,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.490
which is why Aldi's no frill
strategy is so effective."

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.010
From the moment customers
put quarters in their carts

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.450
and walk into the store,

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.090
they can see all the
ways Aldi reduces costs.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.117
Marketing experts say,

00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:27.360
"These tactics all signal to the customer

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.570
that milk, eggs, and
bread are cheaper at Aldi

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.750
because of its efficiency
rather than its quality,

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.120
though, the results of its
cost-cutting strategies

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.760
aren't always popular with customers.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.370
In a survey of roughly 6,500 people,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:42.720
Aldi scored on par with,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.030
or slightly lower than many of its peers

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.453
in categories like store
cleanliness and item availability.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.720
But there's another key tactic grocers use

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.560
to aid their low-cost
images, Known Value Items.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.300
These are staple household products

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.120
whose prices people often remember.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.430
As Aldi has expanded across the country,

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.220
other grocers have reduced
their prices in response,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.620
particularly on these types of items.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.300
- When Aldi comes in,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.140
it prompts other retailers
to look at their prices again

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.380
and kind of, you know, surgically examine

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.920
how to adjust what they
sell and for how much.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.020
- [Narrator] That's because
when people see lower prices

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.250
for staple goods,

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.300
they infer that the business
offers low prices all around.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.310
And when it comes to value for dollar,

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:33.480
Aldi ranked highest in the
customer survey at 96%,

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.330
but stores can only
compete on price so much

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.460
without hurting their profit margins.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:39.810
So what does Aldi use

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.300
other than price point
to draw on customers?

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.610
It's private label items.
(can thudding)

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.520
90% of Aldi's stock is private label,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:48.930
like its other strategies,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.180
this reduces costs for the company.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.370
- We take all of our buying power

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.710
and we put it into the seven most popular

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.650
or common salad dressings
that a customer might buy.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.920
That allows us to deal
directly with manufacturers

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.350
and make sure that we
get the best cost price.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:05.183
- [Narrator] Analysts say that,

00:04:05.183 --> 00:04:07.440
Trader Joe's uses private-label items

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.810
to differ from competitors
by creating items

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.300
that customers can only find there,

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.160
like its Ube Mochi Pancake Mix,

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.260
and Butternut Squash Mac &amp; Cheese.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.690
But at Aldi, you'll
find private-label items

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:19.830
that look quite similar

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.410
to the most well-known national brands.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.380
Instead of Wheat Thins,
Aldi has Thin Wheat.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.467
According to analysts,

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:28.410
"This is because Aldi wants customers

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.990
to think they're essentially
getting the same product,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.150
but at a significant discount."

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.360
- There was a time when
the perception was,

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.520
you know, they're a generic brand

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.470
and, you know, they're just cheaper,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.440
but not as good as the national brands,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.170
and those days are over.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:47.040
- [Narrator] As consumers felt

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.780
the lasting impacts of inflation

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:51.660
sales of store brand products increased.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.760
From 2018 to 2022,

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.490
there was a 34% increase in industry-wide

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.870
private label sales,

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.003
totaling more than $57 billion.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:02.280
- While some other grocers

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.770
might have private label offerings,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.870
this is the existence of what we do.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.120
- [Narrator] Aldi has
thrived during recessions.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.880
The 2008 Financial
Crisis sparked its growth

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.090
and decision to add around
100 new stores per year.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.740
- What was really interesting is

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.410
we never lost any of those customers,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.360
and as the economy improved

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.050
and we had a really long
run of economic improvement,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.350
Aldi grew throughout that
entire period of time.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.410
- [Narrator] And now as inflation
drains American's wallets,

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.390
Aldi continues to grow.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:34.440
- In the beginning,

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.480
that was sort of their target audience,

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.280
people who, you know, wanted bargains,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.370
and wanted to buy food for low prices.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.480
But in recent years,

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.660
I think they've really
expanded that audience

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.950
to also go after people who
want convenient shopping,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.600
who want the necessities,

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.098
and wanna be in and out of stores.

00:05:55.098 --> 00:05:57.765
(playful music)

